{
  "requirement": "Carries an I/O failure or a diagnostic for invalid source input, such as an unknown language or invalid UTF-8 in a request for text.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub enum SourceError {\n    Io(std::io::Error),\n    Invalid(String),\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Debug for SourceError;\nimpl Display for SourceError;\nimpl Error for SourceError;\nimpl From<std::io::Error> for SourceError;\nimpl From<SourceError> for std::io::Error;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The structural obligations in `requirements.sdk.sources.SourceError` are the enum shape and the listed trait impls. Each has a marked root:\n- The enum declaration `pub enum SourceError { Io(std::io::Error), Invalid(String) }` is `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#enum[SourceError]`. It carries `#[derive(Debug)]`, which satisfies `impl Debug`.\n- `impl Display` is `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#impl[SourceError/std::fmt::Display]`.\n- `impl Error` is `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#impl[SourceError/std::error::Error]`.\n- `impl From<std::io::Error> for SourceError` is `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#impl[SourceError/From<std::io::Error>]`.\n- `impl From<SourceError> for std::io::Error` is `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#impl[std::io::Error/From<SourceError>]`.\n\nEach root has an {impl} marker for the requirement. The remaining text, \"Carries an I/O failure or a diagnostic for invalid source input\", is covered by the two variants. I did not read the bodies of the `Display` and `From` impls; the obligations I checked are the declared types and trait impls. No other structural obligation needs an implementation root.\n\nbehavioral: Obligations and the tests that cover them:\n\n- **`Io(std::io::Error)` variant, `From<std::io::Error>`, and `Display` for it.** `source_bytes_error_kinds_and_language_names_round_trip` in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/source_defaults.rs` builds `SourceError::from(io::Error::other(\"unreadable source\"))`. It asserts the result is `SourceError::Io(_)` and that it displays as \"unreadable source\" through `&dyn std::error::Error`. That also shows `Error` is implemented.\n- **`From<SourceError> for std::io::Error`.** The same test converts the `Io` error back and asserts the message is preserved. It converts `Invalid(\"invalid location\")` and asserts `ErrorKind::Other` and the message \"invalid location\".\n- **`Invalid(String)` variant and `Display`.** `invalid_source_errors_display_the_diagnostic` asserts that `Invalid(\"unsupported source language\")` displays exactly that diagnostic, through `&dyn Error`.\n- **Invalid UTF-8 in a request for text.** In the round-trip test, `binary.text()` on the bytes `[0xff, 0xfe]` is matched against `Err(SourceError::Invalid(message))` with a non-empty message.\n- **Unknown language.** The round-trip test also calls `sources.search_structure` with an unknown `LanguageId` (`CustomLang`). It asserts `Err(SourceError::Invalid(message))` and that the message contains \"CustomLang\".\n- **`Debug`.** Nothing asserts on `Debug` output. The requirement states no format for it, so there is no observable obligation beyond the trait being implemented. `public_exports_match_the_appendix` checks that the SDK compiles with its consumer and that its exports match the appendix.\n\nI found no behavioral obligation without a test, and no assertion that contradicts the requirement.",
